ble Hub messages at 12 Hz. Stable sender-to-seat association precedes validated action frames. Gameplay RNG and native cosmetic RNG are separate. No global timescale is used for the time brake.\n\nThe FORMATION kit supplied the mechanical starting point: Catmull arrivals, surviving moving formations, limited shots and prediction-based demonstration aiming. The custom simulation replaces the sample's alternating turns and viewport-dependent geometry. Canonical bounds remain 640 × 360 in every browser and shape.\n\n## Focused engine changes\n\nOnly \"'`vendor/engine/my-3d2dge.js` is shipped. The agent and module editions are absent from the artifact.\n\n1. `Settings` supports `persist:false`: ignore inherited raw values and exclude these keys from saved settings. Developer tuning is session-only but uses native validated sliders and group reset.\n2. `Settings` specs support `hidden:true` in the ordinary Settings list. Explicit native custom-screen rows still display them. This keeps developer mutations out of regular/hosted preference screens while preserving native Dev Mode controls.\n\nHub session.js remains byte-for-byte upstream.\n\n## Distinct game defects found by verification\n\n| ID | Discovery | Severity | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | TV title screenshot and action bounds | Usability | Reduced title typography and panel spacing, intentionally scrollable shell panel; every title action fits 1080p. |\n| 2 | Browser mouse/native-input test | Input | The first pointer move had no prior anchor and could not acquire targeting. First deliberate observed movement now acquires it; keyboard/stick clears the target, later jitter stays ignored. |\n\nTest-fixture corrections (not counted as game defects): synthetic enemy omitted required route data; a test assumed a floating point timer crosses exactly 2.0000 seconds; native shell has both Back navigation and Back action so test selectors must specify one. Failed dependent tests are not separate defects.\nEOF\ncat > docs/authoring.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Author a wave\n\nEdit \"'`src/content/campaign.js`, then `npm run build`. Run `npm test` from this development folder. A wave is data; renderer and simulation need no new branches.\n\n```js\n{\n  name: '\"'Compound chaos', ships: 26, hp: 3,\n  routes: [1, 0, 2], speed: 38, interval: 1.14,\n  mix: ['mine', 'dart', 'hunter', 'dart', 'roller', 'mine'],\n  cue: 'MAKE A GAP. THEN TAKE THE SHOT.',\n  line: 'You are a very expensive rounding error.',\n  color: '#ac99ff', hints: [[12, 'Intercept before you commit.']]\n}\n\"'```\n\n`ships` is the authored membership, `hp` hull integrity, `routes` indexes into the common-plane Catmull paths, `speed` the cruising formation phase speed, `interval` seconds between attack cues multiplied by live attack-interval tuning. `mix` cycles actual weapon families, `cue` the first three-second playfield instruction, `line` a subtitled syllable-voice trigger and `hints` time/text pairs. Shared defaults supply first attack at 4.8 s, arrival spacing 0.18 s, groups of eight and warning tuning. Fleet kills decide completion, rather than a filler timer. Extend validation'\"'s eight-wave expectation if building a different campaign length.\n\nThe four weapons use separate behaviors in the simulation: limited-rate seekers, falling darts, warned committed predictive diamonds and finite bounded ground crosses. Rollers reserve a refuge, never overlap another roller, and suppress other scheduled attacks aimed into that refuge. Add a weapon family by supplying simulation behavior, eligibility/scoring/charge, warning art and audio, plus focused state tests.\n\nFinale phases are in \"'`BOSS_PHASES`: HP, open/closed durations, interval, weapon mix, accent and line. Weak-point schedules use hostile time, so warning and vulnerability slow together during a brake. Players, shots, recovery intervals and the authoritative clock use normal time.\n\nPause → Dev Mode exposes jumps to all waves and boss phases, restart, invulnerability, hitboxes, trajectories, AI, a one-tick step while paused, simulation speed, refill, individual weapon spawns and ten native live tuning sliders. Opening it marks the run before any record can be saved. Reset uses native setting groups; inherited nonbaseline tuning marks every new run as modified.\n\n`node tools/test.mjs` is the one quiet entry point. Optional `state`, `browser` or `network` arguments narrow a rerun to changed coverage. Tests use canonical actions, and the full-campaign success test never sets victory or skips a phase.\nEOF\ncat > README.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Attack of the Time Bandit Boss\n\nOpen the single HTML one folder above this development directory. It includes the full engine, Hub client, PeerJS library, art, content, menus and code audio. Solo needs no network and works through \"'`file://`. Online co-op uses public PeerServer signaling and WebRTC; it requires internet access and compatible NATs.\n\nA/D or arrows to move, Space / mouse to fire, Shift / right mouse for the charged brake, Escape to pause. Standard pad: left stick / D-pad, Cross / RT fires, Square / LT brakes, Options pauses. Touch: relative drag, auto fire, emergency brake and context button. Pause → Dev Mode has all testing and balance tools.\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnpm test\n```\n\nThe quiet test command uses the installed Python Playwright environment, not a downloaded browser. `node tools/test.mjs state`, `browser` and `network` rerun selected coverage. Build metadata lives in `artifacts/build.json`; evidence is in `artifacts/*-results.json` and `docs/verification.md`. Root `PROGRESS.md` records work across context compactions.\n\nSee `docs/research.md`, `docs/authoring.md`, and `docs/build-notes.md`. Exact dependency revisions and licenses are in vendor and the in-game credits. No runtime image or audio files are required.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
